meta {
  name: Get token
  type: http
  seq: 1
}

post {
  url: {{custodianUrl}}/token
  body: json
}

headers {
  Content-Type: application/json
}

body:json {
  {
    "scope": "{{scope}}",
    "authorization_details": [
      {
        "type": "http://fhir.ch/ig/ch-umzh-connect/context",
        "identifier": "{{serviceRequestRef}}"
      }
    ]
  }
}

script:post-response {
  if (res.body && res.body.access_token) {
    bru.setEnvVar("accessToken", res.body.access_token);
  }
}

docs {
  The full L2 flow in one call: the key custodian signs the private_key_jwt
  assertion AND runs the client_credentials exchange at the auth server, then
  returns the token response. The access token is saved to `accessToken` and
  carries this party's SMART scopes, organization_reference claim and the
  service request context as `fhirContext`.

  Body fields are optional: `scope` ({{scope}}) requests specific SMART scopes —
  omit it to fall back to the custodian's DEFAULT_SCOPE. The client is always
  the custodian's CLIENT_ID; any other field (`client_id` included) is a 400.
  `authorization_details` (RFC 9396, type
  `http://fhir.ch/ig/ch-umzh-connect/context`) binds the token to one
  ServiceRequest ({{serviceRequestRef}}); the custodian forwards it to the AS
  as-is, and the gateway checks the resulting `fhirContext`. Send a single
  context entry — OPA rejects tokens bound to more than one. The AS still
  decides what it grants: decode the token to confirm `fhirContext` is present.
}
